The names and the meaning of the operations follow Highway (https://github.com/google/highway), Copyright Google LLC,
which is licensed under the Apache License 2.0 or the BSD 3-Clause License, and core::simd and std of Rust (MIT or Apache License 2.0).

src/simd/math.rs     Began as a port of hwy/contrib/math/math-inl.h (Apache License 2.0). The steps of sin, sin_cos, ln, tanh and atanh,
                and the first step of asin and acos, are still the same. That file names musl for log, and sse_mathfun.h
                (zlib license) and Cephes for sin_cos. No table is from there: tools/coefficients.py computes all. Some
                first coefficients and parts of pi and ln(2) are as in other libraries, since they can't be otherwise.
src/simd/vqsort.rs   Follows the design of hwy/contrib/sort (Blacher, Giesen, Sanders, Wassenberg: "Vectorized and
                performance-portable Quicksort", 2022). No code is from there. The network for 8 values is Batcher's, and that
                for 16 is Green's (1969), in the order of sorting_networks-inl.h (Apache License 2.0).
src/simd/slices.rs   xoshiro256++ and SplitMix64 are by David Blackman, Sebastiano Vigna and Guy Steele, in the public domain.
src/simd/utf8.rs     The check is that of Keiser and Lemire, "Validating UTF-8 in less than one instruction per byte", 2021. Its three
                tables of 16 bytes, the names of their bits and the limits for the end of a block are as in that paper,
                simdjson and simdutf (Apache License 2.0 or MIT).
src/simd/aes_fallback.rs
                The lookups of four bits follow the method of Mike Hamburg, "Accelerating AES with Vector Permute
                Instructions", 2009. No code or table is from elsewhere.

The crate also has these short methods from other projects or has the same steps. They were
written anew in Rust, from memory or after reading. No file, function or table was copied.

Highway         (as above) How some operations are made of others where there is no instruction: partial loads in steps of
                halves, interleaved loads and stores within blocks first, slides of bytes across blocks, shifts that
                round, high products of 64 bits, leading zeros by way of floating point, saturating conversions from
                floating point on x86, compress by tables for eight lanes, masks as four bits for each byte on Arm (first
                described by Danila Kutenin), bit shuffles and sums of absolute differences on AVX-512, and a table of
                entries for dispatch whose first one detects the CPU. Masks from bits, shifts of bytes and by powers of two,
                saturating sums of 32 and 64 bits, comparisons of 128 bits, division by way of floating point, compress of
                16 bits by way of 32, and steps of AES from others. Floating point from unsigned and 64-bit integers by
                halves, for which it names wim (https://stackoverflow.com/questions/41144668 and 34066228).
Hyperscan       (BSD 3-Clause) Sets of bytes by lookups of four bits, there "shufti" and "truffle": ByteSet, ByteClasses.
simdjson        (Apache License 2.0 or MIT) compress of 16 bytes as two halves that a second lookup joins, the
                positions of set bits by compress of 0, 1, 2.. or several at a time, prefix_xor by a carry-less product,
                bitmasks by pairwise sums on Arm, and white space by one lookup.
ggml            (MIT) The sign of one factor moved to the other in dot_add_relaxed for i8.
memchr          (MIT or Unlicense) The shape of the loop of position: one vector, and then four aligned ones at a time.
compiler-rt     (Apache License 2.0 with LLVM exception) The order of the products in powi, so that it gives the results of std.
LLVM            (the same) f32 from u32, f64 from u64 by 2^52 and 2^84, and shifts and rotations by a vector, on x86.
musl            (MIT) Roots in integers, in src/simd/soft.rs: the index of the first guess, and the steps from it. The table is computed.
BearSSL         (MIT) Carry-less products from usual ones.
FP16            (MIT) Rounding to narrow floats by an addition. The way back is by Fabian Giesen, in the public domain.
xxHash          (BSD 2-Clause) Up to three bytes as the first, the middle and the last.
zlib-ng         (zlib license) Matches that reach into themselves by a shuffle, in src/simd/matches.rs. The table is computed.
sm4ni           (MIT) The S-box of SM4 by way of that of AES. The maps are computed.
std_detect      (MIT or Apache License 2.0) Which CPUs and systems report features that can't be used, in src/simd/detect.rs.
                The numbers there are those of the interfaces of the CPUs and systems.

Papers: Granlund and Montgomery, "Division by Invariant Integers using Multiplication", 1994 (Divisor). Boldo and
Melquiond, "Emulation of FMA and correctly rounded sums", 2008 (rounding to odd), with the product of Dekker and Veltkamp.
Payne and Hanek, "Radian reduction for trigonometric functions", 1983 (src/simd/large_angles.rs). Warren, "Hacker's Delight"
(deposit_bits and extract_bits without the instructions, squares of 8 by 8 bits, comparisons, lanes that share a word).
Lemire, Kurz and Rupp, "Stream VByte", 2018 (src/simd/low_bytes.rs). Plank, Greenan and Miller, "Screaming fast Galois field arithmetic",
2013 (LinearMap). Kounavis and Berry, slicing by 8, 2005, and the reduction of
Barrett, 1986 (crc32 and crc32c without the instructions). Câmara, Gouvêa, López and Dahab, "Fast Software Polynomial Multiplication
on ARM Processors Using the NEON Engine", 2013. Muła, Kurz and Lemire, "Faster Population Counts Using AVX2 Instructions", 2018.
Locktyukhin, "Improving the Performance of the Secure Hash Algorithm (SHA-1)", 2010, and Guilford, Yap and Gopal, "Fast SHA-256
Implementations on Intel Architecture Processors", 2012 (the schedules without the instructions). Daemen and Rijmen, "The Design of
Rijndael", 2002 (the inverse of MixColumns). Batcher, "Sorting networks and their applications", 1968. Shepherd and Laframboise,
"Chebyshev approximation of (1 + 2x) exp(x^2) erfc x", 1981. Walczyk, Moroz and Cieśliński, "Improving the accuracy of the fast inverse
square root by modifying Newton-Raphson corrections", 2018 (recip_sqrt_relaxed; tools/reciprocal_root.py computes the numbers).
